From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Wed, 28 Sep 1994 10:47:46 -0400
Subject: [#94-9-568] Omega Point

 price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-9-535:
>>> If we buy Tipler's line about being resurrected into blissful 
>>> heaven by angels we must also accept that other copies of ourselves
>>> will be reincarnated into painful versions of hell, tortured 
>>> forever by sadistic virtual demons.
>> 
>>  Nothing in Tipler's argument requires this,
>
>It does if we believe that *all* possible mental states will be
>resurrected - as Tipler does - since being tortured (forever!) is an
>all-too-possible mental state.  Unfortunately.

 I didn't see any argument that all possible states should or would be
resurrected, only that they could be. What would motivate it? My
understanding is that the Omega Point would resurrect anyone who
would want to be resurrected (although he doesn't address this
explicitly), and that some lesser beings would probably perform
some resurrections for other reasons.

>> The possibilty of a few billion years of torture at the hands of a
>> powerfull foe would deter most people [from being nasty] that I can
>> imagine.
>
>There's quite an assumption here, which Tipler made, that attack is, and
>will remain, easier than defence.  If perfect defense becomes possible
>(perhaps by retreating to a basement universe, for example) then threats
>of retribution aren't going to cut much ice.

 If by defense you mean making a region of spacetime impregnable, that
doesn't prevent someone outside that region from resurrecting and
torturing the guilty person. The concept of a defence against unwanted
resurrections seems confusing.

