Subject: The pitfalls of futurology

Dear netters of the 2020world list:

I am enjoying this list very much ! That's because besides being 
a regular, died-in-the-wool scientist and uniprof (Medical Inform-
atics), I also write a weekly science column in one of the daily
newspapers in my town. I love to write about speculations on the
future, as my readers now, so the Seattle column and the ensuing
comments from the list members are giving me a lot of ideas for
future subjects. 

One of the columns I wrote some time ago touched upon the subject
of futurology. Who remembers Dr. Kahn, the futurologist from a
think-tank in the sixties, who wrote a thick book with predictions
for the year 2000. Well, I have read some parts of the book
again, and it just hilarious ! One of the things Kahn predicted
was that Peru, Iraq, Lebanon and South Vietnam would be ** totally
industrialized countries by the turn of the century, more than
Brazil and China. This shows the inherent dangers of predicting
the future far ahead, such as in 2020.

The main danger lurking for the futurologist (amateur or professio-
nal, it doesn't matter) is the breakthrough, the unexpected and
the revolutionary development no one was able to predict. Extrapo-
lating the present is easy.

There's a story that President Roosevelt assembled a highly
distinguished panel of scientist just before II WW, to produce
some predicions for the next two decades. Well, guess what
happened ? The poor guys were unable to predict, among other
things, antibiotics, the transistor and commercial jetliners.
Not to memtion artificial satellites and a host of other
things deriving thereof.

So, my friends, beware: it's an amusing pasttime, but as the
Chinese say: "The problem with futurology is that it is
impossible to predict the future".

But let's keep discussing.

Renato M.E. Sabbatini, PhD
Director, Center for Biomedical Informatics
State University of Campinas
Campinas, Brazil

