From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Wed, 28 Sep 1994 10:05:22 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-574] Omega Point

Mike Price: 
> But who can feel threatened by a copy being tortured, when they, the
> original, are still alive?

I imagine one would feel as threatened by this, as one would feel
desire to have a copy of oneself live in bliss.

A missing piece of this puzzle is whether there is continuity in 
consciousness.  If I make a copy of myself and "wake" that copy up, 
will life as I experience it continue in the new copy with probability 
1/2?  Zero?  Does that probability depend on the amount of similiarity
or error in the copy?  If zero, then from where does the copy get its 
continuity?  How about if I incrementally destroy the original in the process
of making the copy?  How about if another copy appears somewhere
else in the universe partly by random (eg Omega Point "resurrection"?)

Robin Hanson has made a good economic argument that software
evolutures will end up treating their copies exactly as they treat
themselves; so that for example they would go to great lengths
to avoid a copy being tortured.  Would they go to great lengths
to avoid randomly resurrected copies in an infinite universe from
being tortured?

